    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d335-a1">

                <pb facs="0838" n="716" sameAs="frus:frus1889/pg716"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 187.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bissinger to Mr. Pringle.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-03-20"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 20,
                        1889</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report as the result of
                    the honorable Mr. Straus’ telegram dated Constantinople, the 16th instant, as
                    follows:</p>
                <p>“Grand Vizir instructed valis Beyrouth, Damascus not to disturb American schools
                    to reopen one closed, complaints to be sent to him.”</p>
                <p>That the muchir, in his capacity as acting vali of Beirut, has promptly issued
                    orders to the mutessarif of Latakia to allow the reopening of the recently
                    suppressed American mission school at Istubigo.</p>
                <p>The prompt and efficient action of the honorable minister in securing this most
                    gratifying result for the Latakia mission school is now also invoked for the
                    “Ain Burdhei” school, in the vilayet of Syria, closed in November, 1888 (see
                    last part of dispatch No. 183 of March 4, 1889), as, if permitted to remain
                    closed, it is apprehended that our apparent inaction may be interpreted as
                    indifference on the part of the representatives of our Government to the
                    interests of the mission and encourage the caimakam of Baalbek to renewed
                    aggressions and obstructions.</p>
                <p>The mission associates itself with this consulate in tendering sincere
                    acknowledgements to their respected minister.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Erhard Bissinger.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
